# Chart color and series identity consistency

Tracking issue: https://github.com/KHolodilin/repo-growth-monitor/issues/185
Related: #184 (Traffic metric parity).

Apply this rule when adding or changing analytical KPI, sparklines, graph series, chart legends/tooltips, series selection or chart exports. Existing inconsistencies are tracked by #185; this document does not mean they are already fixed. Keep unrelated work scoped and report pre-existing violations separately.

## Fixed metrics: one semantic identity

- Resolve Views, Visitors, Clones, Unique Cloners, Stars, Forks, Watchers and Contributors through a single shared metric-style registry.
- Bind style to the underlying metric ID, not its display text, translated label or position in an array. Display aliases must resolve deliberately; never merge different measures just because labels are similar.
- Clones and Unique Cloners are different metric IDs and need distinct styles. Contributors / Unique Contributors labels must use their deliberate metric-family mapping without changing their aggregation semantics.
- Reuse that style across the named metric's KPI sparkline, chart line/point, legend swatch, tooltip marker, mobile series marker and PNG export; preserve it between Portfolio, repository and relevant competitor views.
- Do not let chart-library default palette ordering choose a named metric's color. Do not duplicate independent metric hex constants in page components.
- Document the canonical mapping in the shared registry. Prefer intentional established colors when migrating; do not recolor unrelated UI as part of a local fix.
- Keep axes, numeric text and ordinary labels readable and neutral. A colored swatch is sufficient to express series identity.

## Series identity and status mean different things

- Increase/decrease/warning/error/missing colors describe state, not metric identity.
- A delta arrow or change label may use a semantic status color. Do not make the named metric's sparkline change color merely because the trend changes sign.
- Events, unavailable observations and Other use explicit shared conventions. Do not accidentally allocate their neutral/status styles to ordinary metrics.
- Preserve semantic identity in supported themes; theme-specific colors may differ in RGB while staying recognizable and maintaining readable contrast.

## Dynamic query/topic/source/path/repository series

- Key styles by stable domain identity: query/watch/repository ID, full source key or full normalized path, with repository/scope qualification where needed.
- Do not use current row index, selected-array index, sorting order, truncated name or legend position as identity.
- Preserve an existing series style through sorting, selection changes, hide/show, refresh, period changes, responsive transitions and reordering. If scope changes the actual data series, make that identity distinction explicit.
- Handle palette/hash collisions deterministically. Do not recolor existing series each time the selected subset changes.
- For the normal supported visible series count, choose distinct usable colors. If visible series exceed the useful palette, distinguish them with stable line-pattern and marker combinations as well.
- Two concurrently visible series must not have the same complete color + line pattern + marker signature. Match those signatures in chart, legend and tooltip where applicable.
- Keep readable names available. Do not depend on hue alone to distinguish series; preserve marker/pattern/label cues and selection state.

## Required verification for affected work

Use this checklist in the PR validation report. State what was actually checked; do not claim automated enforcement because this Markdown file exists.

- [ ] The same fixed metric uses the shared identity/style in KPI, graph, legend, tooltip/mobile marker and PNG, on all affected screens.
- [ ] Clones and Unique Cloners are distinct; display aliases do not accidentally change the underlying mapping.
- [ ] Named-metric colors do not depend on series order or chart-library defaults; no new independent per-page metric palette was introduced.
- [ ] Delta/status colors remain separate from series colors; a sign change does not recolor the metric sparkline.
- [ ] Sort/reorder, hide/show, refresh, period changes and desktop/mobile transitions preserve existing series styles.
- [ ] Dynamic-series fixtures include intentional collisions and more visible series than palette entries; active signatures remain distinguishable and deterministic.
- [ ] Supported themes keep chart marks and labels readable, with non-color identity cues where needed.
- [ ] Relevant automated regression tests pass and test real consumers: KPI/chart option agreement, aliases, dynamic identity/collisions and status separation. Tests must not merely copy the registry constants into expected constants.
- [ ] Interactively compare affected sibling screens. For the #185 migration cover Portfolio/Repository Traffic and Stats, Search/Topics, competitor graphs and History sources/paths; check mobile and desktop.
- [ ] Record remaining pre-existing violations and scope limitations explicitly rather than marking them passed.

This rule adds review requirements. The shared registry is frontend/src/lib/seriesStyle.ts; consumer regression checks accompany #185. Follow issue-workflow.mdc and issue-execution.mdc when implementation is requested; do not infer permission to merge, release or deploy from this rule.

## Canonical mapping and dynamic assignment

- Views / Stars: #4267b2; Visitors / Forks: #2e8540; Clones / Watchers: #b7791f; Unique Cloners: #7c3aed; Contributors (including Unique Contributors): #c43c3c.
- These colors target the supported light theme; no dark theme is currently exposed by the app. Status arrows retain their separate status palette.
- Dynamic styles use full domain keys (query/watch IDs, repository GitHub ID, source key, full path). Allocate sorted new identities through prepareSeriesStyles before selecting a subset. Never derive identity from rank/table position.
- Assignments are persisted locally in rgm-series-styles-v1. If browser storage is unavailable, the in-memory registry keeps the current session stable. Existing assignments are never displaced by newly discovered identities.
- The first usable palette is followed by unique generated hues and stable line/marker variants. Legend swatches and tooltip markers must use the same identity. Neutral Other and missing observations are reserved explicitly.

## Observations, warnings and interactive legends (issues #196–#201)

- Every main line chart shows a marker for every observed value on mobile and desktop (`showAllSymbol: true`), including zero, peaks and flat sections. Keep semantic color and symbol identity. KPI sparklines are exempt from point markers.
- Missing collection is not zero and is not a rank. Never add neutral scatter dots to encode uncollected days. Connect neighboring real observations through missing slots; do not invent observations.
- An expected missing T−1 snapshot uses the shared triangle with `!` inside the plot, on its date, independently of the selected metric. Never move it into the title or attach visibility to Views. Base collection completeness on snapshot/run metadata, not a competitor disappearing from Top N or a null rank from a successful search. A stored zero is complete data. Do not warn before tracking began or for disabled/not-expected collection.
- Tooltips distinguish known source absence, collection failure, not-executed and partial aggregation. If only absence is known, say no stored snapshot without inventing a failure reason.
- All legends toggle line visibility via label and marker. Preserve the hidden entry for restoring a line, expose keyboard/touch operation and accessible selected state, and retain choices across periods. Keep candidate selection and visible-series controls coordinated.
- Mobile selectors wrap readable complete names into rows without horizontal overflow. All/language controls use content width, consistent padding and a full touch/focus hint when ellipsized.
- Every tooltip, including warnings/events, stays inside the viewport and wraps long content. Mobile uses deliberate taps. Outside tap, scroll, period/metric change and route change dismiss it.

Required validation in every affected PR:
- [ ] Main graph families audited: Portfolio/Repository Traffic/Stats, Search/Topics watch lists, query/topic details, both competitor details, Referrers/Paths History.
- [ ] 320/390 px and desktop, Calendar/Timeline, all supported periods; every observation has a marker and missing slots have none.
- [ ] Zero, missing T−1, not-found rank, partial aggregation, before-first-snapshot and disabled collection fixtures use the correct semantics.
- [ ] Legend hide/restore and selection survive a period switch; long labels remain readable and controls accessible.
- [ ] Tooltip bounds/dismissal verified interactively for values, events and warnings.

- Referrers/Paths History uses the shared 1d/7d/30d/90d/1y/all calendar periods against saved snapshots (#205); do not confuse GitHub’s rolling-window metric with archive retention or silently clamp a selected period to 14 days.
